{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Vasicek1953-filter-15-air",
  "Source": "A. Vasicek, \"Multiple thin dielectric films as mirrors and interference filters,\" Czech. J. Phys. 3, 206-222 (1953). doi:10.1007/BF01687127 - Sec. III b-c, pp. 219-220 (rho'15^2, Fig. 8)",
  "Notes": "Same 15-film interference filter without cover glass (last boundary film-air)\nSpecification: Transmission peak at 550 nm\nReported: p. 220: with air as the last medium the reflectance at 550 nm equals that of the uncoated glass, R = ((1.518-1)/(1.518+1))^2 = 4.23 % (films of total path difference lambda, 2 lambda, ... are absent at that wavelength), so T = 95.8 % (absorption neglected, single surface). Independent TMM check: R = 0.9954 at x' = 300 and 420 deg (660 and 471.4 nm), R = 0.281 at 8250 and 4125 A.\nNotes: Light from air; order symmetric. Rear surface of the glass ignored. Same materials as the cemented version.\nLayers from Sec. III b-c, pp. 219-220 (rho'15^2, Fig. 8); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R 4.23 % at 550 nm; paper R = 4.23 %, that of bare glass.",
